    "lawCode": "Amd §§108, 111 & 118, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6565\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in\nrelation to examining the need for standards for the designation of\ntherapy dogs and the definition and identification of therapy dogs\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThis bill would require the commissioner of agriculture and markets to\nconduct a study regarding the necessity of standards for the\ncertification, training, and evaluation of therapy dogs.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 - Requires the commissioner to examine the need for\nstandards for the licensing, certification, and training of therapy\ndogs clarify, the rights and privileges of therapy dogs and similar\nanimals and the differences between therapy dogs, service dogs,\nemotional support animals and other similar types of dogs and animals.\n\nRequires submission of the report to the Governor, The Temporary\nPresident of the Senate, and the Speaker of the Assembly by October 1,\n2018.\n\nSection 2 - Updates the definition of therapy dog to clarify that they\ndo not qualify as service dogs\n\nSection 3 -Authorizes municipalities to grant a license tag for\ntherapy dogs\n\nSection 4 - Makes it illegal to knowingly put a false identification\ntag on a therapy dog\n\nSection 5 - Establishes the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThough they serve an increasingly important role in society, there is\nno presently defined process for certifying therapy dogs in the State\nof New York. Therapy dogs do not fall under State or Federal (through\nthe Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990) law as \"service dogs\",\nwhich have certain protections and qualifications.\n\nCurrent law defines a therapy dog as a dog \"trained...for such\npurpose\" as providing for emotional and physical health, but does not\nset forth any training or credentialing requirements. As such, any dog\nmay be described as a \"therapy dog\". Regardless of whether or not they\nhave received proper training. This presents situations wherein\nuntrained animals presented as so-called therapy dogs can be in the\npresence of the public and other, trained, service animals. There have\nbeen instances of trained service animals being maimed by untrained\nanimals.\n\nThis legislation will require the Commissioner to study this issue and\nmake recommendations regarding standards that should be imposed\nregarding the training and privileges of these animals in the state.\nThe commissioner should work with the communities and organizations\nmost affected by this issue in determining the need for standards.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNone\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n\nNone\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
